  "query_preview": "..Today through Friday - strong high pressure aloft\nwill develop over the Great Basin while a low forms off the\nnorthern Baja coast. The result will be rapidly increasing\neasterly flow aloft over our region. There will also be tighter\neasterly surface pressure gradients as a surface high plunges down\nthrough the Rockies and plains. Expect northeast to east winds to\nincrease through this period, especially along/west of terrain \nfeatures. The period of strongest winds currently looks like later\ntonight through Friday morning, including areas around Flagstaff,\nSedona, and the I-17 corridor from Flagstaff down to the Hwy 179 \ninterchange. Gusts in these areas may reach 40-50 mph, with most \nother areas staying in the 30-40 mph range. Otherwise, expect dry \nand continued cooler than normal conditions through Friday.\n\nSaturday and Sunday - Some moisture should move back into the \nregion from the southeast by the weekend, and a low (10-25%) \nchance of showers and thunderstorms is included in our latest \nforecasts. Rain chances may increase as we get closer, depending \non the track of the weak upper low as it ejects northeastward late\nin the weekend and possible interaction with deeper moisture \nassociated with tropical systems far to the south off the coast of\nMexico. While most of the ensemble guidance continues to keep the\ndeeper moisture to our east through Sunday, a small percentage\n(including latest operational EC) bring it farther west, so this \nwill be something to monitor. Daytime highs look to stay below \nnormal through the weekend. \n\nMonday through Wednesday - ensemble forecasts show deeper low \npressure diving south along the West Coast, settling over or just \nwest of central/southern California by Tuesday or Wednesday. This \nwill bring the potential for deeper moisture from the south to \ninteract with the low over our region, and our rain chances have \nincreased to 30-50% Tue/Wed. Daytime temperatures will remain \ncooler than average for mid October.",
